What was imported
------------------
This is NOT a hand-picked subset. GreenBoost only needs
`auto_round.auto_scheme.gen_auto_scheme`/`delta_loss`/`register` +
`auto_round.schemes` (the AutoScheme quality-tier search + calibration
engine), but `auto_round/__init__.py` eagerly imports the package's full
feature surface (AutoRound's main compressor class, every algorithm
config: RTN/SignRound/SignRoundV2/AWQ/Hadamard/SpinQuant) — and Python
always executes a package's `__init__.py` before any of its submodules,
so there is no way to import just the 4 target files without triggering
that eager chain. A full dependency trace (AST-walking every
`auto_round.*` import transitively, from the 4 files above as roots)
found 139 files are actually reachable — verified by import, not just
counted: `import auto_round` (this vendored copy) succeeds end-to-end.
That is what's vendored here: every file the real import graph touches,
preserving the exact `auto_round`/`auto_round_extension` package layout so
internal relative/absolute imports resolve unchanged.

NOT vendored: `test/`, `docs/`, `examples/`, `scripts/` (upstream's own
test/doc/example trees) — this repo's own tests
(tests/test_gb_quant_calib_delta_loss.py) cover the GreenBoost-side
integration, same exclusion policy as llama.cpp/NOTICE and
synapse_engine/NOTICE.

New runtime dependencies this pulls in (declared in
synapse_engine/pyproject.toml): `accelerate`, `py-cpuinfo` (both new;
`datasets`, `pydantic`, `tqdm`, `numpy`, `torch`, `transformers` were
already direct or transitive dependencies of the torch engine). This is
the exact "drags in accelerate/datasets" outcome a narrower 4-file vendor
was meant to avoid — confirmed unavoidable for THIS upstream package
layout (its `__init__.py` is not lazy), not a scope creep on GreenBoost's
side. `calibrate_with_prompts()` (gb_quant_calib.py) imports this tree
lazily, inside its own function body only, gated behind
`sensitivity_source="delta_loss"` / `GB_QUANT_CALIB=delta_loss` — never at
gb_quant.py's own module level, and never touched by the zero-data
default calibration path.

Local modifications
--------------------
Every local patch to the vendored tree MUST be appended below, in order,
so future contributors can tell upstream code from GreenBoost's own
changes at a glance (same convention as third_party/gemlite/NOTICE).

- `auto_round/__init__.py`: removed the unconditional `monkey_patch()`
  call at import time. Upstream's `monkey_patch()`
  (`auto_round/utils/common.py:monkey_patch_transformers`) globally
  patches the PROCESS-WIDE `transformers` module (rebinds
  `transformers.modeling_utils.no_init_weights`, wraps
  `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained`'s kwargs, patches RoPE init) as a
  side effect of merely `import auto_round`. GreenBoost imports this tree
  lazily inside gb_quant_calib's opt-in delta-loss path, in the SAME
  process as gb_synapse_fallback/synapse_engine's own transformers usage
  — a calibration helper must never silently alter global transformers
  behavior for every other consumer sharing that process. Verified: with
  the patch, `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained.__func__`
  is identical before and after `import auto_round` (bound-classmethod
  objects themselves always differ across separate attribute accesses —
  `__func__` is the correct identity check, not the bound method).
  `auto_round.monkey_patch()` itself is untouched and still callable by
  any future caller that specifically wants AutoRound's own patches; none
  do today.

No other files were modified — everything else is a verbatim copy at the
pinned commit.
